title:: Event types
summary:: Different ways that an Event can "play"
categories:: Streams-Patterns-Events>Events
related:: Classes/Event


An link::Classes/Event:: responds to a code::play:: message by evaluating code::~play:: in the event, and the default behaviour of code::~play:: is determined by the value of code::~type::.footnote::To see how event types are normally invoked, here is a slightly simplified version of the default definition of  code::~play:: as defined in the Event class:

code::{ ~eventTypes[~type].value(server); }::

The function uses the value of ~type to select a function from the Dictionary held in ~eventTypes. ::


code::
a = (play: { ~word.scramble.postln }, word: "hello word");
a.play;

a = (type: \note, freq: [1310, 1321]); // choosing a play function by specifying type
a.play;
::


The collection of eventTypes can be readily extended using link::Classes/Event#*addEventType#*addEventType:::

code::
Event.addEventType(\test, { "Your word is: ".post; ~word.scramble.postln });
(type: \test, word: "annahme").play;
::


section::Currently existing event types:

Note:: this documentation is incomplete. ::

subsection:: \note

Instantiate a synth on the server, with specified arguments, and later to free it. The choice of link::Classes/SynthDef:: is specified using the code::\instrument:: key. This event type is what code::Event.default:: returns.

code::
(degree: [0, 5, 7, 11]).play;
::

Actually plays this event type:

code::
(type: \note, degree: [0, 5, 7, 11], instrument: \default).play;
::

There are two alternative keys for code::\degree::: code::\note:: and code::\midinote::.
Each of the three keys behaves differently.
(See link::Tutorials/A-Practical-Guide/PG_07_Value_Conversions#Pitch conversions:: and link::Reference/Literals#Scale Degrees::.)

subsubsection:: Basic Examples
code::
/* The default scale: C major scale starting from MIDI note 60 */

Pbind(\scale, Scale.major, \degree, Pseq((0..7), 1), \dur, 0.25).play;
Pbind(\degree, Pseq([0,  1,  2,  3,  4,   5,  6,  7], 1), \dur, 0.25).play; // The same result as above.
// \degree:          0,  1,  2,  3,  4,   5,  6,  7 (= octave higher than 0)
// solmization:      do, re, mi, fa, sol, la, si, do
// pitch class:      C,  D,  E,  F,  G,   A,  B,  C
// \midinote:        60, 62, 64, 65, 67,  69, 71, 72 // 72 = 60 + 12
// \note:            0,  2,  4,  5,  7,   9,  11, 12 // 12 = 0 + 12

// Using \midinote, the code in the first line can be transcribed as:
Pbind(\midinote, Pseq([0,  2,  4,  5,  7,  9,  11, 12] + 60)).play;

// or as:
Pbind(\midinote, Pseq([60, 62, 64, 65, 67, 69, 71, 72])).play;

// Alternatively, using \note:
Pbind(\note,     Pseq([0,  2,  4,  5,  7,  9,  11, 12])).play;

// However, it should not be written as:
Pbind(\note,     Pseq([0,  1,  2,  3,  4,  5,  6,  7])).play;


/* Other scales starting from MIDI note 60 */

Pbind(\scale, Scale.minor, \degree, Pseq((0..7), 1), \dur, 0.25).play;
// \degree:          0,  1,  2,      3,  4,   5,      6,      7
// solmization:      la, si, do,     re, mi,  fa,     sol,    la
// pitch class:      C,  D,  E flat, F,  G,   A flat, B flat, C
// \midinote:        60, 62, 63,     65, 67,  68,     70,     72
// \note:            0,  2,  3,      5,  7,   8,      10,     12

Pbind(\scale, Scale.harmonicMinor, \degree, Pseq((0..7), 1), \dur, 0.25).play;
// \degree:          0,  1,  2,      3,  4,   5,      6,      7
// solmization:      la, si, do,     re, mi,  fa,     sil,    la
// pitch class:      C,  D,  E flat, F,  G,   A flat, B,      C
// \midinote:        60, 62, 63,     65, 67,  68,     71,     72
// \note:            0,  2,  3,      5,  7,   8,      11,     12
::

subsubsection:: More Examples
code::
Pbind(\degree, Pseq([2.1, 3])).play
// 2 = E; 3 = F
// 2.1 = 2s = degree 2 with a sharp (E sharp = F) = 3

Pbind(\degree, Pseq([5, 5.1, 5.9, 6])).play
// 5 = A; 6 = B
// 5.1 = 5s = degree 5 with a sharp (A sharp)
// 5.9 = 6b = degree 6 with a flat (B flat)
// in 12 equal temperament: A sharp = B flat
// 5.1 = 5.9

Pbind(\degree, Pseq([5, 5.2, 6])).play
// 5 = A; 6 = B
// 5.2 = 5ss = degree 5 with a double sharp (A double sharp)
// in 12 equal temperament: A double sharp = B
// 5.2 = 6

Pbind(\degree, Pseq([5, 5.8, 6])).play
// 5 = A; 6 = B
// 5.8 = 6bb = degree 6 with a double flat (B double flat)
// in 12 equal temperament: A = B double flat
// 5 = 5.8

Pbind(\degree, Pseq([5, 5.7, 4.1, 4])).play
// 4 = G; 5 = A; 6 = B
// 5.7 = 6bbb = degree 6 with a triple flat (B triple flat)
// 4.1 = 4s = degree 4 with a sharp
// in 12 equal temperament: B triple flat = G sharp
// 5.7 = 4.1

Pbind(\degree, Pseq([0, 3, 3.1, 5.5, 5])).play
// 3 = F; 4 = G; 5 = A; 6 = B
// 5.5 = 6bbbbb = degree 6 with a quintuple flat (B, B flat, A, A flat, G, G flat)
// Caution: bbbbb and sssss are not supported as literals. The maximum allowed is four.
// 3.1 = 3s = degree 3 with a sharp
// in 12 equal temperament: B with a quintuple flat = F sharp
// 3.1 = 5.5
::

subsection:: \set

used to set parameters of some already-running node(s).

code::
a = (degree: 3, sustain: 40).play;
fork { 10.do { (type: \set, id: a[\id], \degree: [0, 5, 8, 11].choose).play; 0.3.wait } };
::

(See also: note in link::Classes/Pmono:: helpfile)


subsection:: \group

creates a new group
optional parameters:
table::
## ~id || node ID, or node object
## ~group || outer group id or object
## ~addAction / ~lag / ~timingOffset || determine how and when the group is created
::

Example:
code::
(type: \group, id: 2).play					// create a group with nodeID 2
(type: \note, freq: 500, group: 2).play		// play a synth in that group
::


subsection:: \midi

send note parameters to midi device
parameters:
table::
## ~midicmd || A link::Classes/Symbol::, for the MIDI command to issue
## ~midiout || A link::Classes/MIDIOut:: object
## ~chan || The MIDI channel number (0-15)
::

See link::Tutorials/A-Practical-Guide/PG_08_Event_Types_and_Parameters#MIDI output:: for details on available midicmds.

subsection:: \on

play synth, ~id must be specified
subsection:: \off
release synth (or free if no gate)
subsection:: \kill

free synth

subsection:: \rest

do nothing for a specified amount of time

subsection:: \composite

perform any number of event types, given as ~types

code::
MIDIClient.init;
m = MIDIOut(0);

// should play a synth *and* an external MIDI note simultaneously
(type: \composite, types: [\note, \midi], midiout: m, degree: 0, dur: 3).play;
::

subsection:: \bus

write ~array to control buses starting at ~out

subsection:: \audioBus
allocate ~channels consecutive audio buses

subsection:: \controlBus

allocate ~channels consecutive control buses

subsection:: \alloc

allocate ~bufnum with ~numframes and ~numchannels

subsection:: \allocRead

load a file from ~path, starting at ~firstFileFrame, reading ~numFrames sample frames

subsection:: \cue

cue a file for DiskIn, with ~bufferSize frames

subsection:: \free

free ~bufnum

subsection:: \gen

send ~gencmd to ~bufnum

subsection:: \load

load ~filename starting at ~frame into ~bufnum

subsection:: \read

subsection:: \table

load ~amps directly into a buffer

subsection:: \sine1

generate a buffer from ~amps

subsection:: \sine2

generate a buffer from ~freqs, ~amps

subsection:: \sine3

generate a buffer from ~freqs, ~amps, ~phases

subsection:: \cheby

generate a waveshape buffer from ~amps

subsection:: \setProperties

sends setter messages to ~receiver for each key in ~args that has a nonNil value in the link::Classes/Event::.

subsection:: \tree

creates a tree of groups. ~tree can be an array of nodeIDs, and may contain associations to further nested arrays.

subsection:: \phrase

instead of playing a single synth from a link::Classes/SynthDef:: with ~instrument, it looks up a link::Classes/Pdef:: and plays a cluster of sounds.

